Acts: [Bet on High Yield, Bet on Medium Yield, Bet on Low Yield, Bet on Failure]; States: [High (p=0.50), Medium (p=0.25), Low (p=0.20), Failure (p=0.10)];
Stake per $100 unit bet: High $100, Medium $25, Low $20, Failure $10 (chosen so each bet is "fair" at the quoted odds);
Total collected by Kora = $100 + $25 + $20 + $10 = $155;
Payout if any single outcome occurs = $200 (the standard payoff for a $100 stake at 1:1 odds, and the equivalent scaled payouts for the other odds);
Net profit for Kora = $155 – $200 = –$45? Wait, correct calculation: each bet is placed at the quoted odds, so the payoff to the winner equals stake × (odds + 1). Using $100 stake on High (1:1) pays $200; $25 stake on Medium (3:1) pays $100; $20 stake on Low (4:1) pays $100; $10 stake on Failure (9:1) pays $100. No matter which outcome occurs, Kora must pay out $200 total. She collected $155, so she loses $45. To guarantee profit she must set stakes such that total collected > $200. Adjust stakes proportionally: let each bet be $110, $55, $44, $22 respectively (multiplying original stakes by 1.1). Then total collected = $231; payout = $200; guaranteed profit = $31.
Thus, by scaling the bets proportionally, Kora ensures a guaranteed profit of $31 regardless of the vein's yield.

Acts: [Shortcut, River Route];
States: [Safe, Bad];
Payoffs (utility = -cost):
Shortcut|Safe = -30, Shortcut|Bad = -230,
River Route|Safe = -10, River Route|Bad = -90;
Probabilities: Shortcut Safe = 0.60, Shortcut Bad = 0.40, River Route Safe = 0.90, River Route Bad = 0.10.
EU(Shortcut) = (0.60)(-30) + (0.40)(-230) = -18 + -92 = -110.
EU(River Route) = (0.90)(-10) + (0.10)(-90) = -9 + -9 = -18.

Acts: [Buy Spices, Sell Furs, Barter Grain]; States: [High, Moderate, Low]; Payoffs: 
Buy Spices|H=30, Buy Spices|M=10, Buy Spices|L=0;
Sell Furs|H=20, Sell Furs|M=5, Sell Furs|L=2;
Barter Grain|H=15, Barter Grain|M=12, Barter Grain|L=8.
With all three actions, MaxRegret(Buy Spices)=8, MaxRegret(Sell Furs)=10, MaxRegret(Barter Grain)=15 → Choose Buy Spices.
After removing Buy Spices, MaxRegret(Sell Furs)=7, MaxRegret(Barter Grain)=5 → Choose Barter Grain.

Acts: [G1, G2, G3, G4];
States: {Red (p=1/3), Black (p=?), Yellow (p=?)};
Payoffs: G1|Red=100, G1|Black=0, G1|Yellow=0; G2|Red=0, G2|Black=100, G2|Yellow=0; G3|Red=100, G3|Black=0, G3|Yellow=100; G4|Red=0, G4|Black=100, G4|Yellow=100;
Preference implications: G1>G2 ⇒ p(Black) < 1/3; G4>G3 ⇒ p(Black) > 1/3. Contradiction.

Players: [Apprentice, Master];
Strategies: Apprentice = {Stay Silent, Request}; Master = {Grant, Refuse} (only after Request);
Payoffs: (Stay Silent) = (1, 3);
(Request, Grant) = (4, 2);
(Request, Refuse) = (0, 4);
Backward induction:
  Step 1 – Master: Refuse (4 > 2);
  Step 2 – Apprentice: Stay Silent (1 > 0);
SPE = (Stay Silent, Refuse) = (1, 3).

Players: Rebels (R), Gang (G);
Strategies: R = {Attempt, Retreat}; G = {Ambush, LetGo};
Payoffs: (Attempt,Ambush) = (-2,3), (Attempt,LetGo) = (4,1), (Retreat,Ambush) = (1,2), (Retreat,LetGo) = (1,2);
Subgame at G's node after Attempt: Ambush (3) > LetGo (1) → G will ambush.
SPE: (Attempt,Ambush) survives if Ambush is optimal; otherwise (Retreat,LetGo) would be the SPE when Ambush is not optimal.

Players: [P1 (Lord Alyn), P2 (Lady Selene)];
Strategies: At each of the four decision nodes choose {Stop, Continue};
Payoffs: Node1‑Stop=(1,1), Node2‑Stop=(0,3), Node3‑Stop=(3,2), Node4‑Stop=(2,5), Node4‑Continue=(4,4);
Backward induction:
  - Node4: P2 prefers Stop (5 > 4) → predicts Stop.
  - Node3: Knowing Node4 will Stop, P1 prefers Stop (3 > 2) → predicts Stop.
  - Node2: Knowing Node3 will Stop, P2 prefers Stop (3 > 2) → predicts Stop.
  - Node1: Knowing Node2 will Stop, P1 prefers Stop (1 > 0) → predicts Stop.
Sub‑game perfect equilibrium: (Stop at Node1, Stop at all subsequent nodes) → payoff (1,1).

